[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (5) The permission of the court must be obtained before making a voluntary interim payment in respect of a claim by a child or protected party. ‘Child’ and ‘protected party’ have the same meanings as in rule 21.1(2). [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (6) The court may adjust any interim payment whether or not any payment has yet been made (voluntarily or under a previously made order). The court may in particular—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (a)order all or part of the interim payment to be repaid;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (b)vary or discharge the order for the interim payment;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (c)order a defendant to reimburse, either wholly or partly, another defendant who has made an interim payment. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (7) The court may make an order under paragraph (6)(c) only if—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (a)the defendant to be reimbursed has claimed against the other defendant for a contribution, indemnity or other remedy; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (b)where the claim to which the interim payment relates has not been discontinued or disposed of, the circumstances are such that the court could make an interim payment order under this Section. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (8) Where a defendant has made an interim payment, and the amount of the payment is more than their total liability under the final judgment or order, the court may award them interest on the overpaid amount from the date when they made the interim payment. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (Practice Direction 40B, paragraphs 6.1 to 6.4 provide further information concerning adjustment of the final judgment sum.)

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] 25.21. —(1) The claimant may not apply for an interim payment order before the end of the period for filing an acknowledgment of service applicable to the defendant against whom the application is made. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (Rule 10.3 sets out the period for filing an acknowledgment of service.)

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (2) The claimant may make more than one application for an interim payment order. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (3) A copy of the application notice must be served at least 14 days before the hearing of the application and must be supported by evidence. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (4) If the respondent wishes to rely on written evidence at the hearing, they must file the written evidence and serve copies on every other party to the application at least 7 days before the hearing. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (5) If the applicant wishes to rely on written evidence in reply, they must file the written evidence and serve a copy on the respondent at least 3 days before the hearing. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (6) This rule does not require written evidence to be filed if it has already been filed, or to be served on a party on whom it has already been served. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > SECTION V Interim Payment Orders] (7) The court may make an order under this rule without an application by any party if it makes the order when it disposes of the claim or any part of it. ## 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (a)the sum of money sought by way of an interim payment;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (b)the items or matters in respect of which the interim payment is sought;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (c)the sum of money for which final judgment is likely to be given;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (d)the reasons for believing that the conditions set out in rule 25.23 are satisfied;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (e)any other relevant matters;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (f)in claims for personal injuries, details of special damages and past and future loss; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (g)in a claim under the Fatal Accidents Act 1976( 4 ), details of the person(s) on whose behalf the claim is made and the nature of the claim. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (2) Any documents in support of the application must be exhibited, including, in personal injuries claims, any medical report. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] 25.23. The court may only make an interim payment order where any of the following conditions are satisfied—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (a)the defendant against whom the order is sought has admitted liability to pay damages or another sum of money to the claimant;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (b)the claimant has obtained judgment against that defendant for damages to be assessed, or for another sum of money (other than costs) to be assessed;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (c)it is satisfied that, if the claim went to trial, the claimant would obtain judgment for a substantial amount of money (other than costs) against the defendant from whom they are seeking an interim payment, whether or not that defendant is the only defendant or one of several;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (e)the claimant is seeking an order for possession of land (whether or not any other order is also sought), and the court is satisfied that, if the case went to trial, the defendant would be held liable (even if the claim for possession fails) to pay the claimant a sum of money for the defendant’s occupation and use of the land while the claim for possession was pending; or

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (f)there are two or more defendants and the order is sought against any one or more of them, and—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (i)the court is satisfied that, if the claim went to trial, the claimant would obtain judgment for a substantial amount of money (other than costs) against at least one of the defendants (but the court cannot determine which); and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (ii)all the defendants are either public bodies; or insured in respect of the claim; or are defendants whose liability will be met by an insurer under section 151 of the Road Traffic Act 1988( 5 ), or an insurer acting under the Motor Insurers Bureau Agreement, or the Motor Insurers Bureau where it is acting itself. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] 25.24. —(1) Where in a claim for personal injuries there is a contested application for interim payment of damages, the defendant must obtain a certificate from the Secretary of State (as defined in rule 36.20(1)(e)) where the claim—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (a)falls under the heads of damage set out in column 1 of Schedule 2 to the Social Security (Recovery of Benefits) Act 1997( 6 ) (‘the 1997 Act’) in respect of recoverable benefits received by the claimant set out in column 2 of that Schedule, or includes damages in respect of a disease for which a lump sum payment within the definition in section 1A(2) of the 1997 Act has been, or is likely to be made; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (b)the defendant is liable to pay a recoverable amount (as defined in rule 36.20(1)(c)) to the Secretary of State. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (2) A copy of the certificate must be filed at the hearing of the application. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (3) The order must set out the deductible amount (as defined in rule 36.20(1)(d)). [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] (4) The payment made to the claimant must be the net amount but for the purpose of calculating the payment after the final judgment, the gross amount of the interim payment must be used. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.22. —(1) An application for an interim payment order must be supported by evidence dealing with—] 25.25. The fact that a defendant has made an interim payment, whether voluntarily or by court order, shall not be disclosed to the trial judge until all questions of liability and the amount of money to be awarded have been decided, unless that defendant agrees. ## SECTION VI Security for Costs

## 25.26. —(1) A defendant to any claim may apply for security for their costs of the proceedings. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.26. —(1) A defendant to any claim may apply for security for their costs of the proceedings.] (Part 3 provides for the court to order payment of sums into court in other circumstances. Rule 20.3 provides for this Section to apply to counterclaims or other additional claims.)

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.26. —(1) A defendant to any claim may apply for security for their costs of the proceedings.] (2) An application for security for costs must be supported by written evidence. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.26. —(1) A defendant to any claim may apply for security for their costs of the proceedings.] (3) Where the court makes an order for security for costs, it must determine the amount of security, and direct the manner and time within which the security must be given. ## 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (a)it is satisfied, having regard to all the circumstances of the case, that it is just to make such an order; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (b)either an enactment permits the court to require security for costs, or one or more of the following conditions apply—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (i)the claimant is resident out of the jurisdiction;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (ii)the claimant is a company or other body (whether incorporated inside or outside England and Wales) and there is reason to believe that it will be unable to pay the defendant’s costs if ordered to do so;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (iii)the claimant has changed their address since the claim was commenced with a view to evading the consequences of the litigation;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (iv)the claimant failed to give their address in the claim form, or gave an incorrect address;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (v)the claimant is acting as a nominal claimant, other than as a representative claimant under Part 19, and there is reason to believe that they will be unable to pay the defendant’s costs if ordered to do so;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (vi)the claimant has taken steps in relation to their assets that would make it difficult to enforce an order for costs against them. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] 25.28. The defendant may seek a security for costs order against a person other than the claimant, and the court may make such an order, if—

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (a)the court is satisfied, having regard to all the circumstances of the case, that it is just to make such an order;

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (b)the person has assigned the right to the claim to the claimant with a view to avoiding the possibility of a costs order being made against them, or has contributed or agreed to contribute to the claimant’s costs in return for a share of any recovery in the proceedings; and

[PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (c)the person is someone against whom a costs order may be made. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] 25.29. —(1) The court may order security for costs of an appeal against an appellant, or a respondent who also appeals, on the same grounds as it may order security for costs against a claimant. [PART 25 – INTERIM REMEDIES AND SECURITY FOR COSTS > 25.27. The court may make an order for security for costs if—] (2) The court may also make such an order where the appellant, or the respondent who also appeals, is a limited company and there is reason to believe it will be unable to pay the costs of the other parties to the appeal should its appeal be unsuccessful.
